A||||15||sqoach||Got my radiator back from the shop||||9||
Z||000000||sqoach||04-12-2001||03:52 PM||||The reason I have a sad face is because they said, and here's a quote, "There was some crap in it, but not enough to cause a problem."<P>So I feel that I just wasted nearly $60 to have them check it, rot it out, and get new gaskets for it. I haven't put it back in my car yet. Planning on doing that in a few minutes.<P>He also told me it may be the head gasket that's causing the overheating. But isn't there some sure signs to tell if the head gasket is going?<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">  <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily overheating)||||reg||9
Z||000001||StuGood||04-12-2001||06:14 PM||||No lament for money spent.  Take solace in the fact that you know your radiator's definitely in good shape now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Whenever I get a "new" used car, I often have its radiator rodded out whether it needs it or not, just as a precaution.<P>As far as the head gasket is concerned, I haven't followed your posts closely, but have you checked your spark plugs for signs of  coolant (no cost check)?  After that, check your compression, if you've got / can borrow a gauge.  <P>||||reg||7
Z||000002||drednot||04-12-2001||06:16 PM||||If I were you, I would take it to midas and have the presure test that engine.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||04-12-2001||06:24 PM||||Sqoach:<P>Head-gasket leaks aren't always apparent. I have my suspicions about the head-gasket on my Formula, hoping new radiator fixes the problem. If not, the only thing left is bad head gasket, which will be taken care of soon since I have 2.8 rebuild in the works. <P>Gasket replacement shouldn't be too difficicult on a 2.5L.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rex||04-12-2001||09:40 PM||||sqoach,<BR>      I agree with Fiero 56 a head gasket<BR>leak sometimes can go undetected for some time. However if its bad enough to be causing a heating problem some other symptoms could be but not limited to<BR>1. coolant in the oil<BR>2. oil in the coolant<BR>3.steam coming from the exhaust<BR>4.with the radiator cap removed, excess pressure forcing coolant out and or bubbles<BR>seen in the coolant. I hope this is helpful<BR>good luck and let us all know.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jay||04-12-2001||09:56 PM||||If you look under your oil cap or radiator cap you should see a whitish brown foam.  If it is there, then you've got a problem.  It happened to my first 84 Fiero.  Looked like dirty whip cream.<P>Jay<BR>86 GT Northstar <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GTZ34||04-12-2001||10:16 PM||||Sqoach,  I think you are at the point where you need the right tool for the job.  Actually, if you did this in the first place you would probably know what the problem is/was and may have already fixed it.  Well enough of shoulda coulda woulda...  Either rent or but a cooling system tester.  This will allow you to pump-up your system and see where any coolant is blowing out.  Just don't exceed 100 lbs (just kidding).  You should pump it up to between 15 & 20 lbs based on what your radiator cap is rated.  If you have a leak, it will leak down and you should be able to see it. If you suspect the block, head or head gasket take the spark plugs out and look inside the cylinders as you are doing the test.<BR>BTW - we need a system to start a tool swap.  By a system I mean a method that someone wouldn't mind shipping expensive tools to someone they don't know.  Ideas anyone???||||reg||3
Z||000007||sqoach||04-12-2001||10:42 PM||||Wow! Thanks for all the responses, guys. Before I even took my radiator in to get checked, I had my system pressure-tested and we didn't find any leaks. That's why I had my radiator checked. I already have it put in, filled it up, burped it and all that. Haven't driven it yet though. I'm sure it will do fine for a while like it usual does.<P>On my last compression check, all cylinders were between 130 and 140. That was just with cranking it over 4-6 times. I didn't really look at the spark plugs to see what they looked like, since that wasn't on my mind at the time. But I do remember the #4(?) plug had something on it. Didn't think nothing of it. Kinda smelled like oil, but everything seems to smell like that. And when I first did the compression test (brand new tester I bought) on this cylinder, it was pretty low, near 100. Then I did it a few more times until it got to about 140. The rest of them were about the same each time I re-did the test. And I'm also getting (have been for a while) an off-white colored smoke from my tailpipe, and what seems to be an excess of condensation. Also, on my oil cap I found that the oil was a tan color, but just on the cap. The rest of the oil looks just fine. The last couple times I checked the cap it was like this.<P>Anyway, sorry that was so long. Just trying to get everything out that may contribute to this.<P>I forgot to mention that my coolant looks normal.<p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 04-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||04-12-2001||10:45 PM||||If you run a compression test again, and find a low cylinder, put a few drops of oil in, if the pressure rises dramatically, its most likely the rings. If possible, try having a leak-down test of the cylinders performed.<P>Have you done anything with the water-pump? There are two types(at least for teh six)a stamped impeller style, and a cast. According to Ed Parks, stamped doesn't push enough coolant. Stamped style looks sort of like a boat prop, where cast is a disk with fins on one side.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 04-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||RNGCRSH||04-12-2001||11:08 PM||||You have one of 3 problems<P>1. blown head gasket <BR>2. cracked head<BR>3. cracked block<P>I have seen this problem before on too many diferent cars to list. if your oil turns to the tan stuff, even in the smallest quantity, coolant is finding its way into you oil.<P>I had an engine where the head was improperly torqued after a rebuild. It would only let water into the oil when it reached temp then because of loss of pressure in the coolant system the temp would skyrocket. When the head was removed it had a severe warp.||||reg||1
Z||000010||sqoach||04-12-2001||11:35 PM||||fiero56, I replaced the water pump in the Fall. And it was "the good kind." <P>RNGCRSH, I'm definately leaning toward those 3 conclusions. My car has gotten extremely hot at times. I'm planning on a rebuild when I get the money. Hopefully within a month. I'll be able to check it out then. But first I've got to see if my car is doing okay now. I kinda doubt it, but I'll cross my fingers.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Kerri 98||04-12-2001||11:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sqoach:<BR><B>Wow! Thanks for all the responses, guys. Before I even took my radiator in to get checked, I had my system pressure-tested and we didn't find any leaks. That's why I had my radiator checked. I already have it put in, filled it up, burped it and all that. Haven't driven it yet though. I'm sure it will do fine for a while like it usual does.<P>On my last compression check, all cylinders were between 130 and 140. That was just with cranking it over 4-6 times. I didn't really look at the spark plugs to see what they looked like, since that wasn't on my mind at the time. But I do remember the #4(?) plug had something on it. Didn't think nothing of it. Kinda smelled like oil, but everything seems to smell like that. And when I first did the compression test (brand new tester I bought) on this cylinder, it was pretty low, near 100. Then I did it a few more times until it got to about 140. The rest of them were about the same each time I re-did the test. And I'm also getting (have been for a while) an off-white colored smoke from my tailpipe, and what seems to be an excess of condensation. Also, on my oil cap I found that the oil was a tan color, but just on the cap. The rest of the oil looks just fine. The last couple times I checked the cap it was like this.<P>Anyway, sorry that was so long. Just trying to get everything out that may contribute to this.<P>I forgot to mention that my coolant looks normal.<P>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 04-12-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Have you removed the radiator cap to see if there is a volcanic eruption? (obviously I'm not saying to do this when the car is hot)I did the above things like Rex and Jay said,  all checked out ok, until the "volcanic eruption test" Think that was Rex's number 4 ? <BR>Just one more thing to rule out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
